<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Qata'i</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qatai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Qata'i was the capital founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-tulun">Ahmad ibn Tulun</a> around 870 north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>, on the site of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Its name, 'the allotments', came from the quarters assigned to his troops and officials. It held a palace, a hospital, a great hippodrome and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-of-ibn-tulun">Mosque of Ibn Tulun</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 905 they razed the city, sparing only the mosque.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Al-Qata'i lay immediately north-east of al-Askar, the capital that had succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> passed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> around 750. Al-Askar in turn adjoined Fustat, the first Arab settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, founded by Amr ibn al-A'as in 642. The new city was built on the Gabal Yashkhur, a hill that local legend named as the landing place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noah">Noah</a>'s Ark after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">Deluge</a>.</p><p>Ibn Tulun had been sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as governor by the Abbasid caliph and arrived with a military force too large to be housed in al-Askar. He modelled the city to some degree on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, where he had received his military training and where each section was set aside for a particular group. In al-Qata'i, too, areas were allocated to officers, civil servants, particular military corps, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, guards, policemen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> drivers and slaves. The city was meant to expand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>, a thriving market town, not to replace it, and many officials went on living there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The focal point was the great ceremonial mosque. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a> reported that it was needed because the old mosque of Amr ibn al-A'as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> could not hold Ibn Tulun's personal regiment at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayer</a>. Beside it stood the palace, the Dar al-Imara or House of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a>, with a private door giving the governor direct access to the minbar. The palace faced a large parade ground and park with gardens and a hippodrome.</p><p>Ibn Tulun also had an aqueduct built and founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimaristan">maristan</a>, or hospital, in 873, the first such public institution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; an endowment funded both in perpetuity. Military campaigns brought the capital a large income, and many taxes were abolished during his rule. After his death in 884 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khumarawayh">Khumarawayh</a> enlarged the palaces and built irrigation canals and a sewage system.</p><p>Once the city had been razed in 905, the administration returned to al-Askar, which had by then become indistinct from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>. After al-Qahira was founded in 969, Fustat and al-Qahira grew together over the remains of the Tulunid capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site of al-Qata'i lies within present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-of-ibn-tulun">Mosque of Ibn Tulun</a> is its only surviving building. It is still the largest mosque in Cairo by area and is noted for its pointed arches, used two centuries before they appeared in European architecture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Qata'i” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ibn Tulun founded a new capital, <b>al-Qata'i</b>, north of Fustat, with a palace, hospital and the great Mosque of Ibn Tulun, completed in 879 and still standing.</blockquote><a href="/state/tulunid-emirate">Tulunid emirate</a></li></ul></section>
